  "query": "Research the serviceable addressable market (SAM), serviceable obtainable market (SOM), and market reachability for:\n\n**Industry:** Medical Devices / MedTech / Continuous Glucose Monitoring, Neurostimulation, Brain-Computer Interface\n**Solution Category:** Advanced Medical Device Substrates, Flexible Circuit Components, and Electrode Modules for Wearable and Implantable Medical Devices\n**Geography:** North America, Europe (initial); Global (expansion)\n**Business Model:** B2B Component Supply / CDMO (Contract Development and Manufacturing Organization)\n**Target Buyer:** Enterprise OEM Medical Device Manufacturers\n**Problem Context:** LG Innotek (LGIT) has advanced CoF and flexible PCB substrate manufacturing capabilities with 10\u00b5m-level resolution, currently deployed in IT and automotive markets. The company seeks to leverage these capabilities to enter high-value medical device markets\u2014specifically CGM systems, neurostimulation devices, and BCI systems\u2014where thin, flexible, high-density electrode substrates are critical enabling components. The core challenge is determining where in the medical device supply chain LGIT can defensibly participate given its substrate expertise but absence of medical certifications, biosignal IP, and finished-device experience.\n**Market Terms:** wearable biosensor flexible electronics, high-density electrode substrate medtech, neurostimulation electrode array, micro-electrode array medical device, continuous glucose monitoring sensor substrate, medical electronics contract manufacturing, flexible hybrid electronics medical, biocompatible flexible substrate, implantable medical device PCB, thin-film electrode implantable device, brain-computer interface electrode substrate, medical device flexible circuit substrate, value, medical grade FPCB component supply, sources, CDMO medical device electronics, CoF-based medical electrode module, confidence\n**Known Competitors:** CorTec, NeuroNexus, Microprobes for Life Science, Celestica, NeuroXess, Flex Ltd. (Medical CDMO), Dyconex (Precision Flexible Circuits for Implantables), Cirtran / Benchmark Electronics MedTech Division, BrainCo, Nortech Systems\n**Known Segments:** Abbott Diabetes Care (FreeStyle Libre CGM OEM), Abbott Neuromodulation division, Abbott Cardiac Rhythm Management division, Dexcom (CGM OEM), Medtronic Neuromodulation, Boston Scientific neuromodulation, BCI system developers (clinical-stage), Neurostimulation device OEMs\n\nSearch for: \"Advanced Medical Device Substrates, Flexible Circuit Components, and Electrode Modules for Wearable and Implantable Medical Devices route to market\", \"Advanced Medical Device Substrates, Flexible Circuit Components, and Electrode Modules for Wearable and Implantable Medical Devices channel partners\", \"Medical Devices / MedTech licensing requirements\", \"Advanced Medical Device Substrates, Flexible Circuit Components, and Electrode Modules for Wearable and Implantable Medical Devices adoption rate\", \"Medical Devices / MedTech market penetration\", \"Advanced Medical Device Substrates, Flexible Circuit Components, and Electrode Modules for Wearable and Implantable Medical Devices compliance requirements North America, Europe (initial); Global (expansion)\".\n\nResearch and report on ALL of the following. Cite specific sources for every data point.\n\n**A. Route-to-Market & Channel Structure**\n1. **Dominant route-to-market types**: The main commercial routes used in this market \u2014 direct sales, channel partners, marketplaces, OEM, resale, self-serve. Which routes dominate and why?\n2. **Channel share and segment coverage**: How much of the market each route serves, and which customer segments each route reaches best. Quantify where possible.\n3. **Marketplace or platform access requirements**: Public requirements for listing, integration, certification, or participation on key platforms and marketplaces in this category.\n\n**B. Access & Compliance Gates**\n4. **Jurisdiction-specific licensing or compliance requirements**: Publicly documented legal, certification, or compliance requirements that gate access to target geographies or segments in North America, Europe (initial); Global (expansion). These are the filters between theoretical demand and serviceable demand.\n5. **Time and cost to satisfy access requirements**: Typical duration, fees, audits, or approval effort needed to meet these access requirements.\n6. **Reachable ICP counts by subsegment**: Public counts of organizations or buyers matching the relevant firmographic and use-case profile. Search industry directories, public databases, trade associations.\n\n**C. Adoption & Obtainability**\n7. **Analogous adoption-ramp benchmarks**: How quickly similar products or categories reached meaningful adoption. Include time-to-X-customers benchmarks from comparable markets.\n8. **Typical switching timelines**: How long it takes buyers to replace incumbent tools, manual processes, or vendors in this category. Include implementation timelines and procurement cycles.\n9. **Public penetration and attach-rate benchmarks**: Comparable rates for adoption, seat penetration, attach, or conversion in adjacent categories. Search for \"[category] market penetration rate\" and \"percentage of companies using [technology]\".\n10. **Evidence-backed versus speculative markers**: Which assumptions in public sizing are sourced versus inferred. Help separate robust inputs from guesswork.\n\nFor each finding, note whether it applies globally or is specific to North America, Europe (initial); Global (expansion). Distinguish between hard data and analyst inference.",
